# Svelte 5

Svelte 5, not Svelte 4. Runes replace `$:` reactivity. Snippets and `{@render}` replace duplicated markup.

Source: [svelte-5-vs-svelte-4](https://github.com/PatrickJS/awesome-cursorrules/blob/main/rules/svelte-5-vs-svelte-4-cursorrules-prompt-file.mdc) from awesome-cursorrules. Globs are narrowed so this rule stays off Python files. This app uses Vite, not SvelteKit.

## Event handlers

Event handlers are HTML properties, not `on:` directives.

```svelte
<script>
  let count = $state(0);
  let double = $derived(count * 2);

  $effect(() => {
    if (count > 10) console.warn('Too high');
  });
</script>

<button onclick={() => count++}>
  {count} / {double}
</button>
```

Svelte 4 equivalents to avoid:

```svelte
<script>
  let count = 0;
  $: double = count * 2;
  $: {
    if (count > 10) console.warn('Too high');
  }
</script>
<button on:click={() => count++}>{count} / {double}</button>
```

## Rules

- `$state()` marks reactive state. `$derived()` replaces `$:` for computed values. `$effect()` replaces `$: { ... }` for side effects.
- `$state`, `$derived`, `$effect`, `$props`, `$bindable`, and `$inspect` are compiler syntax. Do not import them.
- Import only real Svelte utilities, such as `tick`, `untrack`, `mount`, or `unmount`.
- `onclick={handler}`, not `on:click={handler}`.
- No event modifiers. `on:click|preventDefault={handler}` becomes `onclick={(e) => { e.preventDefault(); handler(e); }}`.
- Props use `$props()`. Bindable props use `$bindable()`.
- Do not add SvelteKit routing, layouts, or load functions. This UI is Vite plus Svelte 5.
